        p("A 1972 canal home on Bird Key with a 760-square-foot concrete deck spalled at the coping from a salt pool. Scope: demolition, a 4-inch compacted limerock base over geotextile, linear deck drain at the cage low side, travertine bullnose coping mortar-set on the beam, tumbled ivory travertine field in a French pattern, concrete curb at the seawall side and the cage line, polymeric sand on a dry day, sealing at 30 days with annual coping sealing on the care sheet. City permit. Planning range: $17,000 to $22,000."),
        "Is travertine a mistake on a Bird Key salt pool?", "Not if the owner commits to sealing the coping every year and rinsing the field; if not, dense concrete pavers or porcelain are the right call. The estimate says which the owner chose and writes the sealing date on the care sheet.",
